=================================================
			Initial idea 
=================================================

Transmigration as the villainess's tulpa.


Starts with the MC's POV as she's first conjured by the Villainess to keep her company
	- Her best friend flaked on her one too many time. This time it was important/meaningful (eg. going together at a fair?). The girl waited, and waited, and waited, and yet her friend never came nor never sent any message explaining why.
	- Villainess is extremely alone.
	- Villainess folds inwards into herself to hold back from crying in public. She thinks how nice it'd be to still have someone with her right now. She imagines her friend sitting next to her, but the thought is too painful so she instead settles for a nebulous mirror of herself.
	- MC comes into being when Villainess injects just a bit of magic into the pretense to help solidify it and make it feel more real. Again, heavy coping.
	- MC realizes they are a thought construct and quickly observe their own nebulous existence: lack of memory beyond the Villainess's immediate ones. Lack of bodily autonomy, as she sees and feels only through the Villainess's senses, even if her avatar is protected in thoughts somewhere next to or in front of the Villainess.
	- MC understands her purpose, however, and tries to play the part of the imaginary friend here to cheer her partner up. She softens the blow, tell her it's not her fault, and distract her by pointing out some of the neat stuff around them or making jokes.
	- Villainess lets herself be pulled into the fantasy (heeeavy coping)
	- As the two have their fun, MC wrestles with the ephemerescence of their new existence. She knows there is more to her, memories of another world. The people and culture around her feel new, exotic when it should have been very familiar to the Villainess. As such, MC deduces she is her own distinct person, but she doesn't know what happens to her after Villainess stops thinking about her. Does she just stop existing? As such, MC is eager in her new role as imaginary friend, if only as a means of grounding herself as much as possible in the Villainess's psyche. Maybe that way she won't disappear when the latter no longer needs her.
	- Villainess eventually gets overwhelmed by the noises, repressed emotions, and the fakeness of her make-belief. MC pulls her to a quiet place and runs her through some basic sensory deprivation techniques to manage the feelings. She welcome the tears, and reiterate that she's done nothing wrong. It's not her fault. She can let it out, she's safe.

Context
	- MC is more or less a fictive from a storybook the Villainess owns about a world without magic.
		- Eventual character development: MC at first was trying to hold onto and reclaim her memories from another world. She thought she was someone before, with unique skills and experiences. But being confronted as a fictive, she comes to accept that who she was before doesn't matter. Yes she has those memories and they shaped her, but she is a being of this world and belongs to it. Latching onto another world, one that is fantasy for all that she knows, causes hurt to Villainess, and that's not something she wants.
	- The Villainess has internalized that magic as a whole make her life worse. As such, she conjured a spirit who had no magic because that's the type of person she feels safe with.
	- MC doesn't have any magic of her own. If and when she fronts, she can technically access Villainess's magic and skills, but Villainess subconsciously refuses her access to these since it would make MC just another person with magic, able to hurt her.
		- Some character development where Villainess eventually relinquishes her hold on magic and allows MC to share in it.

Magic
	- This world has two systems:
		- The Mantle of the anscestors (ie. fighters)
			- One can invoke a spirit of a great warrior to imbue their form. The spirit overlaps the sumuner and bestows their strength in combat
			- Benefits
				- "Perfect" executions of the ancestor's techniques. Think a fighting fame, with frame data and movesets. While donning the mantle, a warrior with be able to execute every move of the ancestor's legacy with milimeter-like precision. This skill doesn't linger after doffing it, though it can be used to train oneself into learning these same moves the old fashioned way.
				- Empowered healing. While the Mantle doesn't absorb any damage while it's active, it causes significant healing the moment it's doffed, or if the warrior would otherwise succomb in battle. The regeneration comes with a massive aura pressure outwards (imagine Berseker vs Dark Saber from Heaven's Feel)
				- Mantles aren't restricted to humanoid body shapes. Any sapient being whose name or title can be invoked and their being prayed to, can bestow a Mantle. Considering how human will get warped by Myth, there are probably Mantles for every body shapes. Note that if your body is too different from a Mantle to track its movement, it can cause physical traumas.
					- quadruped
					- Birdlike
					- serpentine
					- demonic, etc.
			- Restriction:
				- "Perfect" execution elevates the fighting field to a game of rock-paper-scissors and anticipation. Great fighters will have studied their playstyles, know every options in every scenarios, know their opponent's Mantles, and have tactics/traps/punishes to back everything up. It becomes a knowledge game instead of an execution one.
				- The amount of healing depends on the quality of the Mantle, how devoted the warrior is to their anscestors, as well as how well matched the warrior and anscestor are in both ideology and lifestyles. A good match on all three lets the warrior heal from 3 fatal wounds before the Mantle leaves them. Any regeneration of lost limbs costs 1 heal more than a simple death. Resurection from bisection or inceneration costs all 3 at once. Weaker Mantles or mantler-wielders can usually only access 1 or 2 full revives, with variying levels of healing depending on the type of death and wounds incured.
					- A mantle's strength is colour-coded based on the visible spectrum (rainbow colours). The closer they are to violet, the higher the power output and the more lives/recovery one gets. A red mantle is very weak and flimsy.
						- Purple = 3 lives, or 2 lives and limbs, or being cut in half
						- Blue = 2 lives and heavy damage
						- Cyan = 2 lives, or 1 life and limb
						- Green = 1 life and scratch damage
						- Yellow = 1 life or limb
						- Orange = heavy damage, but no life
						- Red = scratch damage
				- The number of Mantles in a single area causes them to resonate with one another and inflicts more and more strain on their wielders, limiting the number that can take part in the same battle. The default is about a 100 meters radius.
					- One mantle causes no strain and can be held leisurely all day long.
					- Two mantles cause high adrenaline spikes, elevated bloodflow, increased metabolism and awareness. The can usually be only maintained in the same space for 5-10min
					- Three mantles cause pain, muscle crisping, overheating, quick dehydration and potentially loss of consciousness. If all three endure the symptoms, one of them is bound to lose consciousness within a minute.
					- Four mantles cause burns, internal bleeding, aneurisms, and death.
				- Anyone can don a Mantle. However:
					- If your body is not flexible or strong enough to do certain movements to completion, the movements will complete regardless of your body's limitation. This can lead to torn muscles and ligaments or broken bones. Body-shape is not relevant here: you can be tall and fight using a short anscestor's Mantle.
					- If your devotion towards the anscestor doesn't earn their respect, the Mantle will be flimsily attached and it risks leaving you the moment you take scratch damage
					- If you do not match the Ancestor's lifestyle, you will never "master" the Mantle, meaning the Mantle won't count for your own legacy. This is the requirement most are fine leaving out though.
				- A doffed Mantle cannot be donned again without rest. At a minimum, 2-4 hours, but if the healing was extensive, the Mantle can be exhausted for a day or two per revive depending on the severity of the wounds.
				- A new Mantle can be created in two ways:
					- Becoming a Myth (see below)
					- Mastering 2 or more Mantles, and devising your own unique style from there.
						- To master a Mantle, one must reach a point where their own physical prowesses stand on par with the Mantle's technique without relying on it, as well as thoroughly understanding the Ancestor's culture and lifestyle. Only then will the Anscestor descend and give you its acknowledgement and blessing.
				- Mantles are the peak of humanity combined with Myth. They don't reach transandantial powers. They can be beatten by regular humans, although it'd be like a squad of mooks taking on Captain America. It's a dreadful position to be in. Most Mantles have layered buffs and reinforcements that make them and anything worn or held very tough. Projectiles need their own forms of enchantments, meaning there is functionally no difference between an arrow from a Mantle and a firebolt. A Mantle's authority ends at the range of their aura, about 100m, but falls off exponentially:
					- 100m is normal pressure, guns and so on (eg. bow)
					- 10m is twice the pressure (eg. javelin)
					- 1m is four times the pressure (eg. sword)
					- 10cm is 8 times, which is the cap. (eg. fists, throws, claws or daggers)
		- The Myths of nature (ie. casters)
			- The whispers of myth are a temptation most can hear yet few recognize as such. They occur when people's beliefs towards you tend towards one unanimous view yet feel shrouded in mystery. You are then presented with a choice: Lean into who the whispers believe you are and earn power from mystique, or ignore the whispers and remain as your own.
			- Benefits:
				- The world bends to make real the understanding of others.
					- For example, a fire juggle that spits fire with flame and alcohol can one day discover they developed a fire breath ability of their own. Yet the more they use it without the circus theatrics, the more they are vilified as demonic/draconic/monstrous, eventually turning them into a dragon spawn.
				- Once a new ability develops, you can use it in ways that solidify a different image than that which the public thinks of you
					- For example, the above fire juggle could spend more time and circus prestidigitation to pretend their strikes are producing flames rather than their mouth. After a while, if the public believes it so, their ability will shift and they'll become a firebender, which is more marketable than a firebreather. Safe from demonhood!
			- Restrictions:
				- Mystery is a definite component of it. The more people understand something or think they understand something, the least one can pull upon Myth.
				- You are not in control of who you'll become as the myth influences you. The only control you have is:
					- Whether to pull upon Myth or not
					- and good PR that matches the person you hope to become
				- Power is a measure of both how quickly you give yourself to myth and how far reaching the belief into is.
					- Widespread belief means you can tap a drop of Myth to fuel significant spells, without much changes at all
					- Low yet intense belief will yeild a trickle of power, yet if you open the valve full on you'll have your moment of glory throwing spells left and right, yet you'll mutate into a creature of myth before long, requiring dispatches.
Consequences:
	- The limitations on 2 Mantles occupying the same areas heavily limits their applications:
		- 1v1 duels
		- Small squads of varying skill sets, where only 2 primary fights can be imbued at once. Usually strike teams composed of 2 fighters and 3 supports, some of which being mages (adventuring team, mercenaries)
			- Note that there can definitely be a rotation of who dons the Mantle in the squad at any given time. A pair of archers donning Archer Mantles to reduces the numbers from affar switching to melee warriors with their own Mantles is a valid strategy.
		- Elite troops at the helm of a section of their army (eg. 1 per battalion)
			- Note that there is strict protocols for who gets to don a Mantle at any given time. While one soldier can keep the Mantle indefinitely vs mooks, that encourages the enemy to field a Mantle of their own, thus exhausting your elite. After their fight, another soldier is encouraged to don their Mantle. The more active time one can field Mantles for, the more effective their battalion.
	- Dragons, faes, demons and monsters have two origins
		- Born from Myth, meaning they were once humans that succombed to greed and power and fell.
		- Born from an ancestor, meaning they are the solidified descendent from the above Myth, and they no longer rely on Myth to fuel their powers. Instead, their own internal logic became consistent with the laws of Nature.
	- Systematized magic has become accepted enough that certain spells, especially those as part of an ancestor's mantle, are seen as pure and uncorruptible. Studies of these is legitimate, if only due to the fact that people believe students of magic to have the capacity to learn them. Internally, though, they are a minor application of Myth in which the person becomes recognized by the world as a Student (the role) and can tap into that role to fuel their spells.
	- "Wild Magic" or "Mystiques" is the term that describes unsanctioned Myth. Due to propaganda and generalized fear towards it, people who become wildly suspected of Wild Magic will inevittably fall faster should they tap inot Myth, because society already expects them to fall.
		- There is an Inquisition that aims to keep this knowledge well-guarded, and that are constantly on the lookout for Mystiques


-------------------------------------------------

The Villainess's Manor
	- Her father is a lord Mystic who tapps into the myth of blood drinkers (vampires). His official term is that of a ghoul. He is powerful, but dark, and knows the rules of Myth and uses them to their fullest extent. It's cost, hovewer, is to feed on the life blood of others. He maintains that secret by feeding on his own children, little drops here and there, and a personal nurse with good restorative magic in his pocket to help the kids recover from blood loss. He's also most proficient with a Mantle
	- Her mother is a lady that's constantly pregnant. She has slight hag tendencies, but hangs out with other hags who do have a craving for youthful biology. She thus uses displacement magic on her children to gather bone and redundant body parts, before the Nurse can heal them. The bones are made into bone meal and cooked into meals to support her network of influence amongst the Myth. If the Lord is the most militarily and magically powerful, the lady is the most well-connected. A calculative spider feasting on its youngs.
	- The nurse is in on all of this. She is the willing Lord's mistress, tolerated by the mother hag due to the utility of her healing magic, but there are frictions between the women regarding kid rearing and the attention of the lord. The nurse is loving, yet detached. She doesn't see any of the kids as hers, and so has no problem sending them to their deaths or cleaning up after the the two. She is the one in charge of the house, rarely leaving like a secret locked away from prying eyes. She was the lord's sweetheart before he married the Lady, though those were foolish powerplays on both sides that cascaded consequences that would tie them to one another in spite of it all.
	- The kids. There is one a year from the Lady and one every other year from the Nurse.
		0y - Male bastard (nurse). Born when the nurse was 26 (the lord is 1 year older, and the Lady 2 at 28 back then). He was taken into the household and raised as an upper servant. He got trained as the Steward's second and help.
		
		3y - Eldest daughter (lady). Following his first son, the Lord took his peerage more seriously and married the Lady to produce an heir. This was done in a rush, as both Lord and Lady know the weight of whispers and cannot afford a scandal. Their eldest daughter was paraded around when she was young, as a means of networking, promoting good PR, and establishing a marriage contract early with another house. She was their jewel.
		
		4y - First son (lady), second bastard (nurse). Between the first bastard and first daughter, the Lord did not sleep with the nurse as he couldn't afford another child with her before an heir. After the first daughter was secured, however, he relaxed his restrictions and started giving the Nurse some attention again, much to the frustration of the Lady. The latter took out her frustration on the two bastards, which start the bone-sampling as the common household abuse. The nurse retaliates by healing her sons and refusing to heal the Lady's daughter and son. As such, the Lady's children grow up with a weak constitution, or at least the appareance of it, which in an household of Myth might as well become reality.
		
		5y - Second son (lady). This is the person who will become the Villainess. The lord is trying to mediate between his wifes at this point, though he has his own problems to deal with. While usually he would alternate between drinking some blood of both to keep the putrid smell of his curse away, the Lady refuses him as a powerplay hoping he'll drain the Nurse dry and be rid of her. Instead, the Lord starts drinking from the Nurse's firstborn. The Nurse, giving him an ultimatum that she's going to quit if her doesn't balance his behavior a bit, forces him to either drink from all his kids in a rotation, or none at all. The Lord, too enamoured to get rid of her for her cheek, complies. This is around the time the Nurse realizes she has more sway with the Lord than the Lady does.
		
		6y - Third son (lady), first daughter bastard (nurse). This marks a big paradigm shift in the household. The lord, to keep things even and try to make peace, orders these two to be raised as twins, both legitimate. He enforces equal treatment on both to assuage his two wifes. It works for the servants and the perception outside the manor, but both wives know which one they each birthed, and they are not happy. The Nurse reluctantly accepts that this will give her daughter a better life than as a servant, yet she doesn't appeciate the fact that her motherhood will be erase. As such, she takes on a more hand-on role of wet nurse; she will be motherly, even if she can't be their mother. The Lady is frustrated that she has to afford the same curtousy to a commoner's child, and is cold to the nurse's daughter while pampering here third son. In doing so, she forgets to give her second son any attention nor protection, creating a significant middle-child syndrome. Unprotected from both Ladies, the Second son ends up suffering the most. His only protection is the Eldest daughter, who forms a close connection with him.
		
		7y- 'third daughter' (lady). Stillborn. The Lady copes with her grief by lavishing her own brood over the rest, even the Second son. Meanwhile, the rest of the household is going about normally. Stillbirths are common, nothing to throw a fuss over.
		
		8-9y - War. The Lord is taken away to do his duties with his men, leaving the house to the Nurse and Lady. The latter drinks more to drown her loneliness, and entertains more social gatherings with her Hag group. The kid's Mantle training is pushed hard now that the main Mantle bearer is away. The first daughter, 5-6y old now, is given her noble birthright and pushed and killed within a Mantle as hard as the trainer and the Lady are willing to push her. She will be the noblest of them all, this brooke no argument.
		
		10y- The lord returns victorious. The honour of combat did him good on his Myth, pushing away the darkness with some true humanity. The blood of the battlefield also helped. Second son is 5yo, and this is the last happy year he remembers. The eldest daughter, 7yo, is a shell of herself. All her optimism is gone after two years of the Lady's grueling training. She can no longer shield the Second son from the in-house intrigue.
		
		11yo - The twins, daughters (lady and nurse). They are born on nearly the same day from each of the two wives. The Myth of the previous twins took, and as such these two look completely identical even if that should be impossible. They have features from all 3 parents, and it's to the point that even the mothers can hardly tell them apart. The Lord celebrates this as a massive success, proof that the PR outside these walls is working. He encourages his children, especially the first daughter, to lean into Myth now more than ever, on the back of their victory. Their house is noble, their Myth is noble, they are worthy and this birth is proof of that.
		
		12yo - Second stillbirth (Lady). Paranoid, she suspects sabotage from the nurse. Tainted drinks, curses, anything. The nurse makes herself sparse as a result. The Lord tries to console the Lady, but after a few months she admits she hears the whispers of "child eater" more than ever. It is maddening. Everyone around her dismiss it as the bearings of a mother in grief, and ignore her very fragile state. They defend the Nurse, which plays into the Lady's paranoia. She has a moment of weakness where she goes and kills one of the twins, thinking she knows exactly which one is hers and which is the Nurse's. The eldest daughter (9yo) catches her in the act and tries to stop her, but gets slapped by the Lady's mantle for her trouble. The Lord has to Mantle up and intervene. The whole household is in grief as a result, yet the Lord orders that not a word of this will reach the outside, or else it's treason. This is the point where the Nurse gives up on the children; she realizes the Lady will kill them all one after the other, and accepts that she's helpless to stop it.
		
		14yo - Fourth son (lady). The Lord no longer sleeps with the Nurse under request of the Lady. A rumor is starting to be established that the Lady can only have sons. This casts some doubt as to the legitimacy of the eldest daughter. The Lady requests a parental test to prove to all that she is indeed her daughter. She also points to the surviving twin as yet another daughter, but then the Nurse counters disinterestly that the Lady should also get a parental test to verify that the latter is indeed hers. The Lady is livid, fearful, and refuses. Everyone suspects that she's killed her own daughter, yet so long as there's no proof, everyone can live in denial. The first daughter however hears the whispers that she's illegitimate. As her training in Myth and Mantle continues, she can do nothing but take bits and pieces into her own being.
		
		15yo - The first daughter, age 12, takes her own life. The whole manor is in shambles over it. Kid are traumatized, staff are silenced, not a word leaves the house. The mariage contract is quickly brought to the front. This tragedy is one too many; if they cannot secure the contract, this whole household is doomed. The first son (11yo) had been raised as 2nd heir in case something happened to the daughter. There are talks of finding another contract, but that would take time and social capital they can't afford. Rather, the first son is brought before them and given a choice: either step into the role of the first daughter, drink in the whispers of Myth and replace her until his own body follows into what people expect of her; or live as himself and bear the risk and weight of the housename failing if no wedding is made in time. If the darkness of the house is revealed, he will have no future of his own. With such immense pressure, the young boy folds and is raised as a woman. The Nurse, aware that this is a risky play and might not work the first time, offers to raise the Second son the same way, as a backup. Either of them may show some promise at pretending to be a girl, which doubles the odds of succeeding. The Lady hates this plan on behalf of the first son (she doesn't care about the Second son) but she's desperate enough to go along with it. The first son is declared publicly dead, yet the 'eldest daughter' still lives, even though now she's a recluse.
		
		16yo - youngest daughter (nurse). She once more has the traits of all three parents, but by this point the Lady is too old to bear children. The Nurse is on the cusp, and this is her last one. The Lord is extremely strict about the safety of their last daughter. The Lady isn't allowed in the same room as her. She will be raised as his own, even if she is illegitimate.
		
		17yo - The Lord and Lady foster a rivalry between first and second son to see who takes the best to Myth and body modifications. First son wears a wig, dresses in dresses and is forced to be called 'she' and use the wrong name. He feels a huge amount of dysphoria. Meanwhile, the 2nd son is already effiminated. He was the one who spent the most time with the eldest daughter before. She was practically his mom and best friend, his shield, protector, inspiration. The second son is sudbued and has never known a mother other than her, and so he effortlessly beats the First son in everything pertaining to acting like the Eldest daughter. Everyone notices, which causes the First son to feel existential dread. He is angry, finding the whole thing unfair, and takes it out by bullying the 2nd son. The 2nd son thus tries to spend most of his time away from the house, with acquaintances and friend. One of them becomes his escape, and he latches onto her very very hard, but she's flaky. She dreams big, has a grandiose vision of the future the 2nd son can't help but be taken by, but whenever they make plans his friend isn't there to follow up.
		
		18yo - Story starts. 2nd son is 13yo, just got ignored one too many time by their friend to completely break down from lack of self-worth. He taps into Myth, pulling on a thin dread of "he's insane, like the whole house" and conjures a headmate to stave off loneliness. The Tulpa is more mature, and female to boot. She's a mix between an idealized version of his late older sister, his flaky best friend, and a character from a fairy tale. Her own identity isn't fully formed yet, but it's not completely human.
		
		19yo - The Lord orders the first son to drop the act, and intead hands it to the 2nd son. They are running out of time; the debutante would be at their late eldest daughter's 18 years of age, which is in 2 years. The first son was never a good fit, but the 2nd son is a natural without even trying, and very in touch with the Myth. The first son breaks down from humiliation, his wig ripped and his dress stripped bare. The lord hands these to the 2nd son, who is internally panicking. It's the first time the Tulpa fronts fully, and while no one understands what their seeing, the mere fact that 'her' maneurisms slip so casually and fittingly into her role makes it so nobody can refute this is the way to go. The nurse gives 'her' a checkup, and the story outside the manor unfolds from there. The great conspiracy.



Character recap
- Lord Vampire
- Lady hag
- Nurse mistress (healing mantle)
- Steward
- Kids by the start of the book (L = lady, N = nurse, B = Both)
	- Alive (9x)
		- 1rst bastard (N, 19). Works as the Stewards's second
		- 1rst son (L, 15) Rivalry with 2nd son. Poses as 2nd son since 1rst is legally dead.
		- 2nd bastard (N, 15) Trained as a soldier, often away from home. Aims for a knighthood/barony
		- 2nd son (L, 14) MC, effiminate, Plural, pretends to be eldest daughter. Very lonely
		-- The twins (boy L, girl N, 13) Thick as thieves. Get a lot of attention from L and N
		- 4th daughter (B, 8) Uncanny mix of features. Lives like a ghost, forgot its other half
		- 4th son (L, 5) L's current focus.
		- 5th daughter (N-B, 3) N's current focus. L is not allowed in the same room as her
	- Dead (4x)
		- Eldest daughter (L, 16). Her death casts a shadow over the whole family
		- Third daughter (L, 12). Stillborn. Nobody talks about her except L
		- Missing twin (B, 8). Killed by L. L doesn't talk about her, but N does
		- 6th daughter (L, 7). Stillborn. Talked about if only in relation to L's fall
- MC's friend - flaky, but a big dreamer with lofty goals, imaginations, and drive. An escape.
	- Knows something is up with them when they see the 2nd son return as the eldest daughter.


-------------------------------------------------

I picture a scene where her betrothed walks in on her in a sorry state or discovers her secret somehow. The 2nd prince would panic and try to justify themself, but it would only worsen the betrothed's worst fears and prejudices. The Tulpa would take over to try to salvage the situation and speak something akin to:

"On the day of our wedding, when the time comes to retreat to our room and consume our aliance, I will bare all of myself to you. I will welcome your inspection. You won't find anything amiss, nothing to hint that I won't be then what I'm meant to be now. All I dare hope is for your patience and understanding in this state of... in-between. And if, once you've assured yourself I am woman in truth in every way that matters, your mind still finds the memory of this moment here and now distasteful... I will accept you taking a mistress. I will welcome her children as my own. I will make every effort to make this alliance a success, if not for your family then at least for my own. And perhaps in your wisdom you may find it in yourself to match my efforts with your own."


In truth, I imagine the Imposter to be as taken by the otome protagonist as any of the male leads. It could even be a point of rapport between her and her betrothed, that she recognises his infatuation, encourages it to the detriment of her own feelings, and when he eventually cues in to her own feelings on the matter they share a quiet moment of understanding that yes, the new saintest is eminently alluring and kissable.